Porting aloha-oe
===============

The renderer is ordinary C.  The only platform-dependent part of audio
output is in src/audio/.  The score, synthesizer, command-line parser,
and WAVE writer do not include native audio headers.

The code requires C11, the C and math libraries, and getopt_long(3).
The Darwin backend also uses C11 atomics.  Unix builds need a monotonic
clock and nanosleep(2); the Windows backend uses the corresponding
Win32 facilities.  The makefile is intended for GNU make and BSD make,
with a Bourne-compatible shell for its recipes.

There is no configure script.  Compiler target macros select a backend.
All backend translation units are built, but their guards leave exactly
one definition of pcm_play().  The make variable SYSTEM selects native
linker flags; it does not change the compiler's target.  Use an appropriate
CC and SYSTEM together when cross-compiling.  Clean the object files
before changing targets or compile-time options.  See INSTALL for commands.

No third-party audio or speech library is needed.  Platform SDKs, system
headers, and the compiler's runtime are build requirements.  They are not
bundled with this distribution.

Status
------

Build and playback have been tested on macOS (arm64).  The other
backends have not yet been tested on their target systems.  Driver
support for the requested format must be checked at run time.

Ports
-----

  darwin.c     AudioQueue, with three interleaved stereo float buffers.
               Links AudioToolbox and CoreFoundation.  A device argument
               is a CoreAudio UID.  Queue drain completion is determined
               from IsRunning after AudioQueueStop(false), not merely
               from a buffer becoming available for reuse.

  oss.c        FreeBSD and DragonFly BSD OSS, using /dev/dsp and the
               SNDCTL_DSP_* ioctls.  Writes signed 16-bit little-endian
               stereo PCM.  Progress uses submitted bytes less GETODELAY;
               SYNC completes the drain.  A device argument is a pathname.

  linux.c      The ALSA kernel PCM interface in <sound/asound.h>.
               Unless given a pathname, opens the first non-HDMI,
               non-DisplayPort playback PCM in /proc/asound/pcm.
               There is no libasound dependency, plugin handling, or
               interpretation of names such as "hw:0".  The device must
               accept interleaved stereo S16_LE at the requested rate,
               5--40 ms periods, and a 50--250 ms buffer.  Hardware format
               negotiation is exact; no software resampler is supplied.
               A nonblocking drain is followed until SETUP state.
               An underrun is an error, rather than a reason to restart
               silently with an altered playback timeline.

  winmm.c      The system waveOut API, linked with -lwinmm.  The default
               device is WAVE_MAPPER; a device argument is a decimal
               waveOut device number.  The port uses prepared PCM16
               buffers and event notification.  Position comes from
               waveOutGetPosition when available, otherwise from completed
               buffers.  Buffer headers and data remain alive until the
               driver releases them.

  netbsd.c     NetBSD 9 or later, using <sys/audioio.h>, AUDIO_SETINFO,
               AUDIO_GETBUFINFO, and AUDIO_DRAIN.  The default pathname
               is /dev/audio.  This port relies on play.seek being a byte
               count, as documented for NetBSD 9 and later.  Progress is
               the track's consumption position; mixing and hardware
               buffering can add latency after it.  The final partial
               block is passed to the blocking system drain.

  openbsd.c    The native audioio API, using AUDIO_SETPAR and AUDIO_GETPOS.
               The default pathname is /dev/audio0.  Playback starts
               after a complete buffer has been filled.  Padding allows
               the final data block to finish before AUDIO_STOP.
               Progress follows the device byte counter.  An underrun
               terminates playback to preserve the lyric timeline.

  null.c       The fallback for an unsupported target.  Reports that live
               playback is unavailable.  Rendering and WAVE export still
               work when --no-play is given, provided the common code
               builds on that target.

On Linux, -DALOHA_USE_OSS selects oss.c instead of linux.c when the system
provides a working OSS device and <sys/soundcard.h>.  This is a compile-time
choice, not an automatic fallback from a busy or incompatible ALSA device.

The direct device backends do not negotiate access with desktop sound
servers.  A busy or inaccessible device is reported as an error.  Choose
an available device or use WAVE export; the program does not stop another
process to acquire the hardware.

Windows builds
--------------

The Windows target uses MinGW-w64, including its getopt_long implementation,
with make and a shell.  The makefile supplies -municode for the wmain entry
point and -lwinmm for audio.  MSVC is not currently supported.

wmain converts the wide argument vector to UTF-8 before parsing options.
An attached console is temporarily set to UTF-8 output and restored on
normal return.  Redirected lyrics remain UTF-8.  File operations still use
the narrow C interfaces; arbitrary Unicode filenames are not supported.
Use ASCII names for WAVE files, CSV files, and stem directories.

The PCM interface
-----------------

src/audio.h is the public interface.  play_audio() is a wrapper around
play_audio_with_progress() with no progress callback.  Both calls block
until playback and normal draining have finished, or an error is reported.

  - Input is interleaved left/right float PCM at the given sample rate.
    A frame contains two samples.  Samples must be finite, with nominal
    range [-1, 1].  The caller owns the buffer and keeps it alive until
    the call returns.  A backend must not change it.

  - A null, empty, or "default" device name chooses the backend default.
    Other names have native meanings described above.  Device selection
    must not change the requested channel count or sample rate silently.

  - Zero means success.  A nonzero return follows a diagnostic on standard
    error.  There is no guarantee that all backends preserve errno.
    A zero-frame request succeeds without opening a device or invoking
    the progress callback.

  - The optional callback runs on the calling thread, never on a real-time
    audio callback thread.  Its argument is the number of frames consumed,
    not the number submitted.  Positions must be monotonic and no greater
    than the input frame count.  A successful nonempty playback reports
    the final frame count after draining.  Callback code should return
    promptly; it must not block while waiting for playback to finish.

src/audio/pcm.h is private to the backends.  pcm.c validates inputs and
provides sample conversion, monotonic time, a short sleep, and monotonic
progress reporting.  Exactly one backend implements pcm_play().  The
16-bit converter clips to [-1, 1] and emits explicit little-endian bytes;
it must not be replaced by host-endian stores on big-endian systems.

Adding a backend
----------------

Put the implementation in src/audio/, under a compiler-target guard.  Add
its object to the makefile and exclude its target in null.c.  Add linker
flags only when the platform requires a system library.  Keep platform
headers out of the renderer and public audio interface.

Check the device format returned by the driver.  Handle partial writes,
interrupted calls, and nonblocking retries where the interface permits
them.  Keep byte counts distinct from sample and frame counts, and check
conversions to narrower driver fields.  Bound waits for stalled playback.
Use the device's consumption position rather than wall time to drive
lyrics.  Do not report normal completion merely because all input has
been queued.

Callback-based interfaces require particular care on failure: neither the
sample storage nor callback state may be freed while the driver still
owns it.  Stop or reset the device, release prepared buffers, and remove
callbacks in the order required by that interface.  Error cleanup must
not report a successful drain.

Run make check after changing common code.  It exercises score and signal
properties without opening an audio device, so it does not validate a new
backend.  A useful eventual playback test is a short clip, followed by a
clip shorter than one driver buffer, checking both drain completion and
monotonic progress.  Report the actual target, compiler, driver, and tests
used when submitting a port.
